```sql
SELECT 
  c.course_name, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT p.person_id) AS num_students
FROM 
  Courses c
  JOIN Student_Course_Registrations scr ON c.course_id = scr.course_id
  JOIN Students s ON scr.student_id = s.student_id
GROUP BY 
  c.course_name
ORDER BY 
  num_students DESC;
```

This query joins the `Courses`, `Student_Course_Registrations`, and `Students` tables to get the course name and the number of registered students for each course. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each course is only counted once, even if multiple students are registered for the same course. The `COUNT(DISTINCT p.person_id)` function is used to count the number of unique students. The results are then grouped by course name and ordered by the number of students in descending order.
```sql
SELECT 
  c.course_name, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT S.student_id) AS num_students
FROM 
  Courses C
  JOIN Student_Course_Registrations SC
  ON C.course_id = SC.course_id
  JOIN Students S
  ON SC.student_id = S.student_id
GROUP BY 
  C.course_name
ORDER BY 
  num_students DESC;
```

This query joins the `Courses`, `Student_Course_Registrations`, and `Students` tables to get the course names and the number of students registered for each course. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each course name is only counted once, even if multiple students are registered for the same course. The `COUNT(DISTINCT S.student_id)` function counts the number of unique students for each course. The results are then grouped by course name and ordered by the number of students in descending order.
