```sql
SELECT DISTINCT T1.UnitPrice
FROM Track T1
JOIN InvoiceLine T2 ON T1.TrackId = T2.TrackId
JOIN Invoice T3 ON T2.InvoiceId = T3.InvoiceId
JOIN Customer T4 ON T3.CustomerId = T4.CustomerId
JOIN Album T5 ON T3.InvoiceId = T5.InvoiceId
JOIN Artist T6 ON T5.AlbumId = T6.AlbumId
JOIN MediaType T7 ON T5.MediaTypeId = T7.MediaTypeId
WHERE T1.AlbumId IN (
  SELECT T1.AlbumId
  FROM Album
  JOIN PlaylistTrack ON Album.AlbumId = PlaylistTrack.AlbumId
  JOIN Playlist ON Playlist.PlaylistId = PlaylistTrack.PlaylistId
  JOIN Track ON PlaylistTrack.TrackId = Track.TrackId
  JOIN InvoiceLine ON Track.TrackId = InvoiceLine.TrackId
  JOIN Invoice ON InvoiceLine.InvoiceId = Invoice.InvoiceId
  JOIN Customer ON Invoice.CustomerId = Customer.CustomerId
  JOIN Employee ON Employee.EmployeeId = Customer.SupportRepId
  WHERE T1.Title = 'What are the distinct unit prices of all tracks'
)
ORDER BY T1.UnitPrice ASC;
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT T1.UnitPrice
FROM Album AS T1
JOIN PlaylistTrack AS T2 ON T1.AlbumId = T2.AlbumId
JOIN Playlist AS T3 ON T2.PlaylistId = T3.PlaylistId
JOIN Track AS T4 ON T2.TrackId = T4.TrackId
JOIN InvoiceLine AS T5 ON T2.TrackId = T5.TrackId
JOIN Invoice AS T6 ON T5.InvoiceId = T6.InvoiceId
JOIN Customer AS T7 ON T6.CustomerId = T7.CustomerId
JOIN Employee AS T8 ON T7.SupportRepId = T8.EmployeeId
JOIN Artist AS T9 ON T8.EmployeeId = T9.ArtistId
JOIN Genre AS T10 ON T9.GenreId = T10.GenreId
WHERE T1.Title = 'Track';
```

This query joins the required tables to get the distinct unit prices for tracks. It uses the `DISTINCT` keyword to ensure that each track is only included once in the result set. The `JOIN` clauses are used to link the tables together based on their foreign keys. The `WHERE` clause filters the results to only include tracks with the title 'Track'.
