SELECT 
  D.name AS department_name, 
  AVG(s.salary) AS avg_salary
FROM 
  department D
  JOIN 
  instructor I ON D.dept_name = I.dept_name
  JOIN 
  teaches T ON I.ID = T.instructor_ID
  JOIN 
  student S ON T.sec_id = S.student_ID
  JOIN 
  course C ON T.course_id = C.course_id
  JOIN 
  section S ON C.course_id = S.course_id
  JOIN 
  (SELECT ID, AVG(salary) AS salary 
   FROM instructor 
   GROUP BY ID) AS s ON I.ID = s.ID
GROUP BY D.dept_name
ORDER BY avg_salary DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the department name and average salary for each department. The `AVG(salary)` function is used to calculate the average salary for each department. The `LIMIT 1` clause is used to return only the department with the highest average salary.
```sql
SELECT 
  DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY Dept_Name ORDER BY AVG(Salary) DESC) AS salary_rank
FROM 
  department
JOIN 
  instructor ON department.dept_name = instructor.dept_name
JOIN 
  course ON instructor.ID = course.instructor_ID
GROUP BY 
  Dept_Name
ORDER BY 
  salary DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query uses the `DENSE_RANK()` function to assign a unique rank to each department based on their average salary. It then selects the department with the highest average salary. The `PARTITION BY` clause groups the departments by `dept_name`, and the `ORDER BY` clause sorts the departments by their average salary in descending order. The `LIMIT 1` clause returns only the department with the highest average salary.
```sql
SELECT 
  i.name, 
  AVG(i.salary) AS avg_salary
FROM 
  instructor i
JOIN 
  department d ON i.dept_name = d.dept_name
JOIN 
  classroom c ON d.dept_name = c.building AND c.room_number = c.room_number
GROUP BY 
  i.name
ORDER BY 
  avg_salary DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query joins the `instructor`, `department`, and `classroom` tables based on the `dept_name` and `building` columns. It then groups the results by `instructor.name` and calculates the average salary for each instructor. The results are ordered by the average salary in descending order and limited to the top row.
```sql
SELECT 
  d.dept_name, 
  AVG(s.salary) AS avg_salary
FROM 
  department d
  JOIN 
  instructor i ON d.dept_name = i.dept_name
  JOIN 
  teaches t ON i.ID = t.instructor.ID
  JOIN 
  student s ON t.ID = s.student.ID
  JOIN 
  course c ON t.course_id = c.course_id
  JOIN 
  section s ON c.course_id = s.course_id
  JOIN 
  (SELECT ID, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary 
   FROM instructor 
   JOIN student 
   JOIN takes 
   JOIN section 
   GROUP BY ID) AS subquery ON i.ID = subquery.ID
GROUP BY d.dept_name
ORDER BY avg_salary DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the average salary of each department. It uses subquery to get the average salary of each instructor, then joins this result with the instructor table to get the instructor's name. The final result is sorted in descending order by average salary and limited to the top department.
