```sql
SELECT DISTINCT 
  r.building, 
  r.room_number
FROM 
  classroom c
  JOIN section s ON c.building = s.building AND c.room_number = s.room_number
  JOIN student t ON s.section_id = t.student_id
  JOIN course c2 ON t.course_id = c2.course_id
  JOIN instructor i ON c2.instructor_id = i.ID
WHERE 
  c2.credits BETWEEN 50 AND 100
ORDER BY 
  r.building, 
  r.room_number;
```

This query joins the `classroom`, `section`, `student`, `course`, and `instructor` tables based on the required relationships. It then filters the results to include only rooms that can accommodate 50 to 100 students. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each room is only included once in the results. The `ORDER BY` clause is used to sort the results by building and room number.
SELECT DISTINCT r.building, c.building, r.room_number FROM classroom c JOIN section s ON c.building = s.building AND c.room_number = s.room_number WHERE s.semester = '2024' AND s.year = '2024' AND c.capacity BETWEEN 50 AND 100
```sql
SELECT 
  D.dept_name, 
  B.building 
FROM 
  department D 
  JOIN 
  department B ON D.dept_name = B.dept_name 
  JOIN 
  classroom C ON B.building = C.building 
  JOIN 
  course K ON C.building = K.building AND C.room_number = K.room_number 
  JOIN 
  student S ON C.building = S.building AND C.room_number = S.room_number 
  JOIN 
  takes T ON S.building = T.building AND S.room_number = T.room_number 
  JOIN 
  section X ON T.course_id = X.course_id 
  JOIN 
  instructor I ON T.instructor_ID = I.ID 
  JOIN 
  course C2 ON I.course_id = C2.course_id 
  JOIN 
  course K2 ON C2.course_id = K2.course_id 
  JOIN 
  course K ON K2.course_id = K.course_id 
  JOIN 
  course K3 ON K2.course_id = K3.course_id 
  JOIN 
  course K4 ON K3.course_id = K4.course_id 
  JOIN 
  course K ON K4.course_id = K.course_id
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT T1.dept_name, T2.building 
FROM department T1 
JOIN classroom T2 ON T1.building = T2.building 
WHERE T1.budget = (SELECT MAX(T1.budget) FROM department T1 
                     JOIN classroom T2 ON T1.building = T2.building 
                     WHERE T1.budget = T2.budget);
```

This query joins the `department` and `classroom` tables based on the `building` column, and then selects the `dept_name` and `building` columns from the `department` table where the `budget` column in the `department` table is equal to the maximum `budget` in the `classroom` table. The result is a list of department names and their corresponding buildings with the greatest budget.
```sql
SELECT 
  s.name AS student_name, 
  SUM(t.credits) AS total_credits
FROM 
  student s 
  JOIN takes t ON s.id = t.student_id 
  JOIN section s2 ON t.section_id = s2.sec_id 
  JOIN course c ON s2.course_id = c.course_id 
  JOIN department d ON c.dept_name = d.dept_name 
  JOIN instructor i ON t.instructor_id = i.ID 
  JOIN teaches t2 ON i.id = t2.instructor_id 
  JOIN time_slot t3 ON t2.semester = t3.semester AND t2.year = t3.year 
  JOIN classroom c2 ON t3.time_slot_id = c2.time_slot_id 
  JOIN classroom c1 ON c2.building = c1.building 
WHERE 
  c.credits = (SELECT MAX(c2.credits) FROM course c2) 
GROUP BY s.name
ORDER BY total_credits DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the student's name and total credits. It uses the `MAX` aggregation function to find the highest credits in the `course` table, and then joins the `takes` table
```sql
SELECT 
  s.name AS student_name, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT c.credits) AS num_credits
FROM 
  student s 
  JOIN takes t ON s.ID = t.student_ID 
  JOIN course c ON t.course_id = c.course_id 
WHERE 
  t.semester = 'Fall' AND t.year = '2022'
GROUP BY 
  s.name
ORDER BY 
  num_credits DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query will return the name of the student with the most credits in the Fall 2022 semester.
