SELECT DISTINCT COUNT(DISTINCT c.course_id)
FROM classroom c
JOIN section s ON c.building = s.building AND c.room_number = s.room_number
JOIN course c2 ON s.course_id = c2.course_id
JOIN student s2 ON s.sec_id = s.sec_id
JOIN takes t ON s.sec_id = t.sec_id AND t.semester = t.semester AND t.year = t.year
JOIN advisor a ON t.i_ID = a.i_ID
JOIN instructor i ON a.s_ID = i.ID
JOIN department d ON i.dept_name = d.dept_name
JOIN course c3 ON c2.course_id = c3.course_id
WHERE c3.dept_name = 'Computer Science'
AND c3.credits > 0
AND c2.prereq_id IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY c3.dept_name, c3.credits
ORDER BY c3.credits DESC;
```

This query counts the number of courses without prerequisites for the Computer Science department, where the credits are greater than 0 and there are no prerequisites. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each course is only counted once, even if it has multiple prerequisites. The `GROUP BY` clause
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT c.title
FROM classroom c
LEFT JOIN section s ON c.building = s.building AND c.room_number = s.room_number
LEFT JOIN course t ON s.course_id = t.course_id
WHERE t.prereq_id IS NULL;
```

This query joins the `classroom`, `section`, and `course` tables based on the `building` and `room_number` columns. It then joins the `course` table with the `section` table based on the `course_id` column. The `LEFT JOIN` is used to include all rows from the `classroom` table, even if there is no matching row in the `section` table. The `WHERE` clause filters out the courses that have prerequisites by checking if the `prereq_id` column is `NULL` in the `course` table. The result is a list of course titles that do not have any prerequisites.
