SELECT 
  f.title, 
  f.film_id, 
  a.first_name ||'' || a.last_name AS actor_name
FROM 
  film f
  JOIN film_actor fa ON f.film_id = fa.film_id
  JOIN actor a ON fa.actor_id = a.actor_id
WHERE 
  COUNT(DISTINCT a.first_name ||'' || a.last_name) = (
    SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT first_name ||'' || last_name)
    FROM film_actor
  )
ORDER BY 
  f.film_id, 
  f.title;
```

This query works by joining the `film` table with the `film_actor` table on the `film_id` column, and then joining the `film_actor` table with the `actor` table on the `actor_id` column. It then counts the number of unique first and last names for each film, and selects the film with the highest count. The result is ordered by film ID and title.
```sql
SELECT 
  a.first_name, 
  a.last_name, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT c.name) AS num_actors
FROM 
  actor a 
  JOIN film_actor fa ON a.actor_id = fa.actor_id 
  JOIN film f ON fa.film_id = f.film_id 
  JOIN category c ON f.category_id = c.category_id 
GROUP BY 
  a.first_name, 
  a.last_name
ORDER BY 
  num_actors DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query joins the `actor`, `film_actor`, and `category` tables to get the number of actors in each movie. It then groups the results by actor and last name, and counts the number of distinct actors in each group. The results are ordered by the count in descending order and limited to the top row.
```sql
SELECT 
  a.first_name, 
  a.last_name, 
  a.actor_id
FROM 
  actor a
JOIN 
  (
    SELECT 
      film_actor.actor_id, 
      COUNT(DISTINCT film_id) as num_film
    FROM 
      film_actor
    JOIN 
      film ON film_actor.actor_id = film.film_id
    GROUP BY 
      film_actor.actor_id
  ) AS subquery
ON 
  a.actor_id = subquery.actor_id
ORDER BY 
  num_film DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

