```sql
SELECT 
  p.Position, 
  AVG(p.Points) AS avg_points
FROM 
  player p
JOIN 
  club_rank cr ON p.Club_ID = cr.Club_ID
GROUP BY 
  p.Position
ORDER BY 
  avg_points DESC;
```

This query joins the `player` table with the `club_rank` table on the `Club_ID` column, and then groups the results by the `Position` column. It calculates the average `Points` for each position using the `AVG` function and returns the results in a JSON object.
SELECT AVG(Position, Points) AS avg_points FROM (SELECT club_id, AVG(Total) AS avg_points FROM club_rank GROUP BY club_id) AS subquery JOIN player ON subquery.Club_ID = player.Club_ID
```sql
SELECT 
  p.Position, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT pr.Club_ID) AS avg_points
FROM 
  player p
  JOIN 
  club_rank pr ON p.Club_ID = pr.Club_ID
  JOIN 
  competition_result cr ON p.Club_ID = cr.Club_ID
GROUP BY 
  p.Position
HAVING 
  pr.Gold > 20
ORDER BY 
  avg_points DESC;
```

This query joins the `player`, `club_rank`, and `competition_result` tables to get the average points for each position. It then filters the results to only include players with a gold medal (i.e., `Gold > 20`). The `COUNT(DISTINCT pr.Club_ID)` counts the number of players in each position, and the `GROUP BY` clause groups the results by position. The `HAVING` clause filters the results to only include positions with more than 20 gold medals. Finally, the results are ordered by the average points in descending order.
SELECT p.Position, AVG(p.Points) AS avg_points FROM player AS p JOIN club_rank AS cr ON p.Club_ID = cr.Club_ID WHERE cr.Gold > 20 GROUP BY p.Position
```sql
SELECT 
  c.name AS Competition_Type, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT COALESCE(cr.Gold, 0) + COALESCE(cr.Silver, 0) + COALESCE(cr.Bronze, 0) AS Total
FROM 
  club 
  JOIN club_rank cr ON club.Club_ID = cr.Club_ID
  JOIN competition_result cr2 ON club.Club_ID = cr2.Club_ID
  JOIN competition c ON cr2.Competition_ID = c.Competition_ID
GROUP BY 
  c.name
ORDER BY 
  Total DESC;
```

This query will return a JSON object with the types of competitions and the number of competitions of each type. The `COALESCE` function is used to handle cases where a column may be missing, and the `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each type is only counted once. The `JOIN` clauses are used to link the tables together, and the `GROUP BY` clause groups the results by competition type. The `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results in descending order by the total number of competitions.
```sql
SELECT 
  t1.type, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT t2.Club_ID) AS num_competitions
FROM 
  club_rank t1
  JOIN club t2 ON t1.Club_ID = t2.Club_ID
  JOIN competition_result t3 ON t1.Club_ID = t3.Club_ID_1
  JOIN competition t4 ON t3.Competition_ID = t4.Competition_ID
GROUP BY 
  t1.type
ORDER BY 
  num_competitions DESC;
```

This query will return a JSON object with the types of competitions and the number of competitions for each type. The `COUNT(DISTINCT t2.Club_ID)` is used to count the number of unique clubs for each type, and the `GROUP BY` clause groups the results by type. The `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results in descending order by the number of competitions.
