```sql
SELECT 
  D.Name AS DepartmentName, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT E.EmployeeID) AS NumberOfEmployees
FROM 
  Department D
  JOIN Affiliated_With A ON D.DepartmentID = A.DepartmentID
  JOIN Physician P ON A.Physician = P.EmployeeID
  JOIN Employee E ON P.EmployeeID = E.EmployeeID
GROUP BY 
  D.Name
ORDER BY 
  NumberOfEmployees DESC;
```

This query joins the `Department`, `Affiliated_With`, `Physician`, and `Employee` tables to get the department name and the number of employees in each department. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each department is only counted once, even if there are multiple employees in the same department. The `COUNT(DISTINCT E.EmployeeID)` counts the number of unique employees in each department. The results are then grouped by department name and sorted in descending order by the number of employees.
```sql
SELECT 
  D.Name AS DepartmentName, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT E.EmployeeID) AS NumberOfEmployees
FROM 
  Department D
  JOIN Affiliated_With A ON D.DepartmentID = A.DepartmentID
  JOIN Physician P ON A.Physician = P.EmployeeID
  JOIN Employee E ON P.EmployeeID = E.EmployeeID
GROUP BY 
  D.Name
ORDER BY 
  NumberOfEmployees DESC;
```

This query will return the department with the most employees. The `COUNT(DISTINCT E.EmployeeID)` function counts the number of unique employees in each department, and the `GROUP BY` clause groups the results by department. The `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results in descending order by the number of employees.
