This query will return a list of role names with the number of unique document types each role has. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each role is only counted once, even if there are multiple employees in the same role. The `COUNT(DISTINCT D.Document_Type_Code)` counts the number of unique document types for each role. The `JOIN` clauses link the `Roles`, `Employees`, and `All_Documents` tables to retrieve the required data. The `GROUP BY` clause groups the results by role name, and the `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results by role name.
```sql
SELECT 
  R.Role_Name, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT D.Document_Type_Code) AS Document_Type_Count
FROM 
  Roles R
  JOIN 
  All_Documents AD ON R.Role_Code = AD.Role_Code
  JOIN 
  Document_Locations DL ON AD.Document_ID = DL.Document_ID
WHERE 
  AD.Date_Stored = (SELECT MAX(D.Date_Stored) FROM All_Documents AD2 WHERE AD2.Document_ID = AD.Document_ID)
