```sql
SELECT DISTINCT T1.document_type_code, T1.sent_date, T1.response_received_date
FROM Documents
JOIN Grants ON Documents.document_type_code = Grants.grant_id
JOIN Organisations ON Grants.organisation_id = Organisations.organisation_id
JOIN Project_Staff ON Documents.document_id = Project_Staff.project_id
JOIN Projects ON Project_Staff.project_id = Projects.project_id
WHERE T1.sent_date = (SELECT MAX(sent_date) FROM Documents) AND T1.response_received_date = (SELECT MAX(response_received_date) FROM Documents);
```

This query joins the required tables to find the staff member who started working on the document with the earliest sent date and received date. The `MAX` function is used to find the maximum sent and received dates for each document. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that only one staff member is returned for each document.
```sql
SELECT 
  p.project_details, 
  p.project_id
FROM 
  Projects p
JOIN 
  Project_Outcomes po ON p.project_id = po.project_id
JOIN 
  Research_Outcomes ro ON po.outcome_code = ro.outcome_code
JOIN 
  Documents d ON po.outcome_code = d.document_type_code
JOIN 
  Grants g ON d.document_type_code = g.document_type_code
JOIN 
  Organisations o ON g.organisation_id = o.organisation_id
JOIN 
  Staff_Roles sr ON p.role_code = sr.role_code
JOIN 
  Tasks t ON p.project_id = t.project_id
WHERE 
  t.date_from <= (SELECT MAX(date_from) FROM Projects) AND 
  t.date_to >= (SELECT MIN(date_from) FROM Projects)
ORDER BY 
  p.project_details;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the project details and the project id. It uses the `MAX` and `MIN` functions to get the latest and earliest dates for the `date_from` and `date_to` columns respectively. The `WHERE` clause filters the results to only include projects that have a date from before the latest project and a date to after the earliest project. The
```sql
SELECT 
  p.project_details,
  COUNT(DISTINCT o.outcome_code) AS num_outcomes
FROM 
  Projects p
  JOIN Project_Outcomes o ON p.project_id = o.project_id
GROUP BY 
  p.project_details
ORDER BY 
  num_outcomes DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query joins the `Projects` table with the `Project_Outcomes` table on the `project_id` column, groups the results by `project_details`, counts the number of distinct `outcome_code` for each project, and orders the results by the count in descending order. The `LIMIT 1` clause is used to return only the top result.
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT p.project_details
FROM Projects p
LEFT JOIN Project_Staff ps ON p.project_id = ps.project_id
LEFT JOIN Tasks t ON p.project_id = t.project_id
LEFT JOIN Documents d ON t.document_id = d.document_id
LEFT JOIN Document_Types dt ON d.document_type_code = dt.document_type_code
LEFT JOIN Grants g ON d.document_type_code = g.document_type_code
LEFT JOIN Organisations o ON g.organisation_id = o.organisation_id
LEFT JOIN Organisation_Types ot ON o.organisation_type = ot.organisation_type
LEFT JOIN Research_Outcomes ro ON p.outcome_code = ro.outcome_code
LEFT JOIN Research_Staff rs ON p.project_id = rs.project_id
LEFT JOIN Staff_Roles sr ON rs.role_code = sr.role_code
WHERE p.outcome_code IS NULL;
```

This query joins the required tables to retrieve the project details where the outcome is null. The `LEFT JOIN` is used to include all rows from the left table, even if there is no match in the right table. The `IS NULL` condition is used to filter out the rows where the outcome is not null.
