This query joins the `Documents`, `Organisations`, `Grants`, and `Project_Outcomes` tables to get the count of documents of each type for each organisation. It then groups the results by organisation and orders them in descending order by the count. Finally, it limits the results to the top organisation with the maximum count.
```sql
SELECT 
  o.outcome_code, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT o.outcome_code) AS num_outcomes
FROM 
  Project_Outcomes o
  JOIN Projects p ON o.project_id = p.project_id
  JOIN Organisations o2 ON p.organisation_id = o2.organisation_id
  JOIN Organisation_Types ot ON o2.organisation_type = ot.organisation_type
WHERE 
  o.outcome_code IN (
    SELECT 
      r.outcome_code
    FROM 
      Research_Outcomes r
    GROUP BY 
      r.outcome_code
    HAVING 
      COUNT(DISTINCT r.outcome_code) = (
        SELECT 
          COUNT(DISTINCT o.outcome_code)
        FROM 
          Project_Outcomes o
        GROUP BY 
          o.outcome_code
      )
  )
GROUP BY 
  o.outcome_code
ORDER BY 
  num_outcomes DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

