```sql
SELECT 
  r.role_code
FROM 
  Staff_Roles r
  JOIN Projects p ON r.role_code = p.role_code
  JOIN Documents d ON p.project_id = d.project_id
  JOIN Grants g ON d.document_type_code = g.document_type_code
WHERE 
  r.role_code = (
    SELECT 
      MAX(CASE 
        WHEN g.role_code = 'STAFF' THEN 1 
        WHEN g.role_code = 'MANAGER' THEN 2 
        WHEN g.role_code = 'DEPUTY' THEN 3 
        ELSE 0 
      END) AS count
  )
ORDER BY 
  r.role_code
LIMIT 1;
```

This query uses a subquery to count the number of times each role appears in the `role_code` column. It then joins the `Staff_Roles`, `Projects`, and `Documents` tables to get the corresponding data. The `MAX` aggregation function is used to find the most common role. The results are then ordered by `role_code` and limited to the top row.
```sql
SELECT 
  role_code
FROM 
  Staff_Roles
WHERE 
  role_code IN (
    SELECT 
      role_code
    FROM 
      Project_Staff
    GROUP BY 
      role_code
    HAVING 
      COUNT(DISTINCT document_type_code) = (
        SELECT 
          COUNT(DISTINCT document_type_code)
        FROM 
          Documents
      )
  )
ORDER BY 
  role_code;
```

