```sql
SELECT 
  C.property_id,
  COUNT(DISTINCT R.resident_id) AS resident_count
FROM 
  Properties P
  JOIN Residents_Services RS ON P.property_id = RS.property_id
  JOIN Residents R ON RS.resident_id = R.resident_id
GROUP BY 
  P.property_id
ORDER BY 
  resident_count DESC;
```

This query joins the `Properties`, `Residents_Services`, and `Residents` tables to get the count of residents for each property. The `COUNT(DISTINCT R.resident_id)` function ensures that each resident is only counted once, even if they have multiple services. The `GROUP BY` clause groups the results by `property_id`, and the `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results in descending order by the count of residents.
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT T1.service_type_code
FROM Organizations O
JOIN Services S ON O.organization_id = S.organization_id
JOIN Services_Services RS ON S.service_id = RS.service_id
JOIN Residents_Services RS2 ON RS2.resident_id = RS.resident_id
JOIN Properties P ON RS2.property_id = P.property_id
JOIN Customer_Events C ON RS2.property_id = C.property_id
JOIN Customer_Event_Notes CN ON C.customer_id = CN.customer_id
JOIN Timed_Status_of_Things T ON C.date_moved_in = T.Date_and_Date
JOIN Things T2 ON T2.thing_id = T.thing_id
WHERE O.organization_name = 'Denesik and Sons Party';
```

This query joins the required tables to get the distinct service types that are provided by the organization with the name 'Denesik and Sons Party'. The `WHERE` clause filters the results to only include rows where the organization name matches.
```sql
SELECT 
  C.customer_id,
  C.details,
  COUNT(DISTINCT RS.Count) AS Count
FROM 
  Customers C
  JOIN Residents_Services RS ON C.customer_id = RS.customer_id
  JOIN Properties P ON RS.property_id = P.property_id
  JOIN Services S ON RS.service_id = S.service_id
GROUP BY 
  C.customer_id, C.Details
ORDER BY 
  Count DESC;
```

This query joins the `Customers`, `Residents_Services`, `Properties`, and `Services` tables based on the foreign keys provided in the schema. It then groups the results by `customer_id` and `Details`, and counts the number of services requested by each resident using the `COUNT(DISTINCT RS.Count)`. The results are ordered in descending order by the count.
```sql
SELECT 
  C.customer_id,
  C.details,
  COUNT(DISTINCT S.service_id) AS Number
FROM 
  Customers C
  JOIN Properties P ON C.customer_id = P.customer_id
  JOIN Residents R ON P.property_id = R.property_id
  JOIN Services S ON R.resident_id = S.resident_id
GROUP BY 
  C.customer_id, C.Details
ORDER BY 
  Number DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query joins the `Customers`, `Properties`, `Residents`, and `Services` tables based on the foreign keys provided in the schema. It then groups the results by `customer_id` and `Details`, and counts the number of unique `service_id` for each group. The results are ordered by the count in descending order and limited to the top row.
