SELECT DISTINCT p.product_id
FROM Products p
JOIN (
  SELECT s.supplier_id, AVG(p.product_price) as avg_price
  FROM Products p
  JOIN Supplier_Addresses sa ON p.supplier_id = sa.supplier_id
  JOIN Suppliers s ON sa.supplier_id = s.supplier_id
  GROUP BY s.supplier_id
) AS sub ON p.supplier_id = sub.supplier_id
WHERE sub.avg_price > (SELECT AVG(p.product_price) FROM Products p);
```

This query joins the `Products` table with the subquery that calculates the average price of all products supplied by each supplier. It then selects the `product_id` from the `Products` table where the average price of the products supplied by the current supplier is greater than the overall average price of all products. The result is a list of product IDs that meet the specified condition.
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT p.product_id
FROM Products p
JOIN (
  SELECT s.supplier_id
  FROM Suppliers s
  JOIN Supplier_Addresses sa ON s.supplier_id = sa.supplier_id
  JOIN Addresses a ON sa.address_id = a.address_id
  WHERE s.supplier_id = 2
  AND p.product_price > (
    SELECT AVG(p2.product_price)
    FROM Products p2
    JOIN Product_Suppliers ps ON p2.product_id = ps.product_id
    WHERE ps.supplier_id = 2
  )
) AS subquery
ORDER BY p.product_id;
```

This query joins the `Products` table with the subquery that calculates the average price of products from the supplier with id 2, and then selects the distinct product ids from the `Products` table where the price of the product is greater than the average price. The result is ordered by the product id.
