SELECT 
  COUNT(DISTINCT C.Client_ID) AS num_bookings
FROM 
  Clients C
  JOIN Bookings B ON C.Client_ID = B.Customer_ID
  JOIN Addresses A ON C.Address_ID = A.Address_ID
  JOIN Ref_Service_Types S ON B.Service_Type_Code = S.Service_Type_Code
  JOIN Services S ON B.Service_ID = S.Service_ID
  JOIN Invoices I ON B.Booking_ID = I.Booking_ID
  JOIN Invoice_Items I1 ON I.Invoice_ID = I1.Invoice_ID
  JOIN Order_Items O1 ON I1.Order_Item_ID = O1.Order_Item_ID
  JOIN Order_Items O2 ON O1.Order_ID = O2.Order_ID
WHERE 
  S.Service_Type_Code = 'Booking' AND 
  C.Customer_Name IS NOT NULL AND 
  C.Customer_Phone IS NOT NULL AND 
  C.Customer_Email_Address IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY 
  num_bookings DESC;
```

This query will return the number of unique bookings for each customer, where the booking is a 'Booking' type service and the customer has a valid email address. The results are ordered in descending order by the number of bookings.
```sql
SELECT 
  COUNT(DISTINCT C.Client_ID) AS Total_Bookings
FROM 
  Clients C
  JOIN Bookings B ON C.Client_ID = B.Customer_ID
  JOIN Addresses A ON C.Address_ID = A.Address_ID
  JOIN Services S ON B.Service_Type_Code = S.Service_Type_Code
  JOIN Products P ON S.Product_ID = P.Product_ID
  JOIN Invoices I ON B.Order_ID = I.Order_ID
  JOIN Invoice_Items I1 ON I.Invoice_ID = I1.Invoice_ID
  JOIN Order_Items O1 ON I1.Order_Item_ID = O1.Order_Item_ID
  JOIN Customers C1 ON O1.Customer_ID = C1.Customer_ID
  JOIN Bookings_Services BS ON O1.Order_ID = BS.Order_ID
  JOIN Services S1 ON BS.Service_ID = S1.Service_ID
WHERE 
  B.Status_Code = 'P'  -- Only consider paid bookings
ORDER BY 
  Total_Bookings DESC;
```

This query counts the total number of bookings made by each client, considering only paid bookings. It joins the necessary tables to achieve this. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each client is only counted once, even if
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT 
  b.Order_Date 
FROM 
  Bookings 
  JOIN 
  Addresses 
  ON Bookings.Store_ID = Addresses.Address_ID
  JOIN 
  Clients 
  ON Bookings.Customer_ID = Clients.Client_ID
  JOIN 
  Customers 
  ON Clients.Address_ID = Customers.Address_ID
  JOIN 
  Bookings_Services 
  ON Bookings.Booking_ID = Bookings_Services.Order_ID
  JOIN 
  Services 
  ON Bookings_Services.Service_ID = Services.Service_Type_Code
  JOIN 
  Invoices 
  ON Invoices.Invoice_ID = Bookings_Services.Order_ID
  JOIN 
  Invoice_Items 
  ON Invoices.Invoice_ID = Invoice_Items.Order_ID
WHERE 
  b.Order_Date IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY 
  b.Order_Date;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the order dates of all bookings. It uses the `NOT NULL` condition to exclude any null values in the `Order_Date` column. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each order date is only returned once, even if there are multiple bookings with the same date. The `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results by `Order_Date` in ascending order
```sql
SELECT 
  b.Order_Date 
FROM 
  Bookings 
  JOIN 
  Addresses 
  ON Bookings.Store_ID = Addresses.Address_ID
  JOIN 
  Clients 
  ON Bookings.Customer_ID = Clients.Client_ID
  JOIN 
  Customers 
  ON Clients.Address_ID = Customers.Address_ID
WHERE 
  b.Order_Date IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY 
  b.Order_Date ASC;
```

This query joins the `Bookings` table with the `Addresses`, `Clients`, and `Customers` tables to retrieve the order date of each booking. It uses the `NOT NULL` condition to exclude any null values in the `Order_Date` column. The results are then sorted in ascending order by `Order_Date`.
```sql
SELECT 
  p.Product_Name, 
  p.Product_Price, 
  b.Actual_Delivery_Date
FROM 
  Products p
  JOIN 
  Bookings_Services bs ON p.Product_ID = bs.Product_ID
  JOIN 
  Bookings b ON bs.Product_ID = b.Product_ID
  JOIN 
  Invoices i ON b.Invoice_ID = i.Invoice_ID
WHERE 
  b.Actual_Delivery_Date IS NOT NULL
  AND i.Invoice_ID IS NOT NULL
  AND b.Status_Code = 'Planned'
  AND b.Actual_Delivery_Date > b.Planned_Delivery_Date
ORDER BY 
  p.Product_Name, 
  b.Actual_Delivery_Date DESC;
```

This query joins the `Products`, `Bookings_Services`, and `Invoices` tables to retrieve the planned delivery dates of bookings where the status is 'Planned' and the actual delivery date is later than the planned delivery date. The results are ordered by product name and actual delivery date in descending order.
```sql
SELECT 
  p.Product_Name, 
  p.Product_Price, 
  b.Planned_Delivery_Date, 
  b.Actual_Delivery_Date
FROM 
  Products p
  JOIN 
  Bookings_Services bs ON p.Product_ID = bs.Product_ID
  JOIN 
  Bookings b ON bs.Product_ID = b.Product_ID
  JOIN 
  Invoices i ON b.Invoice_ID = i.Invoice_ID
WHERE 
  b.Status_Code = 'Planned' AND 
  b.Actual_Delivery_Date IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY 
  b.Actual_Delivery_Date ASC;
```

This query joins the `Products`, `Bookings_Services`, and `Invoices` tables to retrieve the planned and actual delivery dates for each booking. The `WHERE` clause filters the results to only include bookings with a status of 'Planned' and actual delivery dates that are not null. The results are then ordered by the actual delivery date in ascending order.
```sql
SELECT 
  COUNT(DISTINCT C.Customer_ID) AS customers
FROM 
  Clients C
  JOIN Addresses A ON C.Address_ID = A.Address_ID
  JOIN Ref_Service_Types S ON C.Address_ID = S.Address_ID
  JOIN Services B ON S.Service_Type_Code = B.Service_Type_Code
  JOIN Bookings O ON B.Service_Type_Code = O.Service_Type_Code
  JOIN Customers C2 ON O.Customer_ID = C2.Customer_ID
  JOIN Invoices I ON O.Order_ID = I.Order_ID
  JOIN Invoice_Items I2 ON I.Invoice_ID = I2.Invoice_ID
WHERE 
  C.Customer_Name IS NOT NULL AND 
  C.Customer_Name!= '' AND 
  I.Invoice_ID IS NOT NULL AND 
  I.Invoice_ID!= '' AND 
  I2.Order_Item_ID IS NOT NULL AND 
  I2.Order_Item_ID!= ''
ORDER BY 
  C.Customer_Name ASC;
```

