  Bookings b ON bs2.Bookings_ID = b.Bookings_ID
  JOIN 
  Customers c ON b.Customer_ID = c.Customer_ID
  JOIN 
  Addresses a ON c.Address_ID = a.Address_ID
  JOIN 
  Marketing_Regions mr ON a.Marketing_Region_Code = mr.Marketing_Region_Code
  JOIN 
  Ref_Service_Types rs ON b.Service_Type_Code = rs.Service_Type_Code
  JOIN 
  Ref_Payment_Methods pm ON b.Payment_Method_ID = pm.payment_method_code
WHERE 
  b.Status_Code = 'Most Frequent'
GROUP BY 
  p.Product_Name
ORDER BY 
  COUNT(DISTINCT p.Product_Name) DESC
LIMIT
```sql
SELECT 
  pm.payment_method_code,
  COUNT(DISTINCT p.product_id) AS total_orders
FROM 
  Ref_Payment_Methods pm
  JOIN Ref_Service_Types rt ON pm.payment_method_code = rt.service_type_code
  JOIN Products p ON rt.service_type_code = p.product_id
GROUP BY 
  pm.payment_method_code
ORDER BY 
  total_orders DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query will return the most common payment method code for all bookings. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each payment method code is only counted once, even if there are multiple bookings with the same payment method code. The `COUNT(DISTINCT p.product_id)` counts the number of orders for each payment method code, and the `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results in descending order by the count, so the most common payment method code is at the top. The `LIMIT 1` clause limits the result to the top row, which is the most common payment method code.
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT 
  C.Client_Name, 
  C.Customer_Name AS Customer_Name
FROM 
  Clients C
  JOIN Customers ON C.Customer_ID = C.Customer_ID
  JOIN Bookings_Services BS ON C.Customer_ID = BS.Customer_ID
  JOIN Bookings B ON BS.Book_id = B.Book_id
  JOIN Invoices I ON B.Book_id = I.Book_id
  JOIN Order_Items OI ON I.Order_ID = OI.Order_ID
  JOIN Products P ON OI.Product_ID = P.Product_ID
  JOIN Services S ON P.Service_ID = S.Service_ID
WHERE 
  B.Status_Code ='stop'
ORDER BY 
  C.Customer_Name;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the names of the workshop groups that have bookings with status code "stop". The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each customer is only listed once, even if they have multiple bookings with the same status code. The `ORDER BY` clause is used to sort the results by customer name.
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT C.Customer_Name
FROM Clients C
JOIN Addresses A ON C.Address_ID = A.Address_ID
JOIN Bookings B ON C.Address_ID = B.Customer_ID
JOIN Customers C2 ON B.Customer_ID = C2.Customer_ID
JOIN Bookings_Services BS ON B.Workshop_Group_ID = BS.Workshop_Group_ID
JOIN Services S ON BS.Service_ID = S.Service_ID
JOIN Invoices I ON BS.Order_ID = I.Order_ID
JOIN Invoice_Items II ON I.Invoice_ID = II.Invoice_ID
WHERE B.Status_Code ='stop';
```

This query joins the required tables to get the names of the customers who have bookings with status "stop". The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each customer is only listed once, even if they have multiple bookings with the same status.
