This query joins the `Ref_Payment_Methods` table with the `Ref_Service_Types` table to get the service type code for the performer 'Ashley'. It then joins the `Addresses` table with the `Ref_Service_Types` table to get the address associated with the service type. Finally, it joins the `Products` table with the `Addresses` table to get the product name for the service. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that only unique product names are returned.
```sql
SELECT 
  pm.payment_method_code,
  COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id) AS num_orders
FROM 
  Ref_Payment_Methods pm
  JOIN Ref_Service_Types rt ON pm.payment_method_code = rt.service_type_code
  JOIN Addresses a ON pm.address_id = a.address_id
  JOIN Customers c ON a.address_id = c.address_id
  JOIN Bookings b ON c.customer_id = b.customer_id
  JOIN Customers orders ON b.customer_id = orders.customer_id
  JOIN Order_Items oi ON b.booking_id = oi.booking_id
  JOIN Invoices i ON oi.invoice_id = i.invoice_id
  JOIN Invoice_Items ii ON i.invoice_id = ii.invoice_id
GROUP BY 
  pm.payment_method_code
ORDER BY 
  pm.payment_method_code;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the number of orders for each payment method code. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each payment method code is only counted once, even if there are multiple orders with the same payment method code. The `COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id)` function is used to count the number of orders for each payment method code. The results are then grouped by payment method code and ordered by the
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT 
  pm.payment_method_code, 
  COUNT(DISTINCT o.Order_Date) AS num_orders
FROM 
  Ref_Payment_Methods pm
  JOIN Ref_Service_Types rt ON pm.payment_method_code = rt.Service_Type_Code
  JOIN Addresses a ON pm.Address_ID = a.Address_ID
  JOIN Customers c ON a.Address_ID = c.Address_ID
  JOIN Bookings b ON c.Customer_ID = b.Customer_ID
  JOIN Customers orders ON b.Store_ID = orders.Store_ID
  JOIN Order_Items oi ON b.Order_ID = oi.Order_ID
  JOIN Invoices i ON oi.Order_ID = i.Invoice_ID
  JOIN Invoice_Items ii ON i.Invoice_ID = ii.Invoice_ID
GROUP BY pm.payment_method_code
ORDER BY num_orders DESC;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the distinct payment method codes with the number of orders made. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to ensure that each payment method code is only included once in the result set. The `COUNT(DISTINCT o.Order_Date)` counts the number of orders for each payment method code. The results are then grouped by payment method code and ordered in descending order by the number of orders.
```sql
SELECT 
  pm.payment_method_code,
  COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_name) AS customer_count
FROM 
  Ref_Payment_Methods pm
  JOIN Clients c ON pm.payment_method_code = c.payment_method_code
GROUP BY 
  pm.payment_method_code
ORDER BY 
  customer_count DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query will return the payment method code used by the most orders, along with the count of customers who have used that payment method. The `DISTINCT` keyword ensures that each payment method code is only counted once, even if multiple customers use the same payment method. The `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results in descending order by the count of customers, and the `LIMIT 1` clause returns only the first row, which is the most common payment method.
```sql
SELECT 
  pm.payment_method_code,
  COUNT(DISTINCT p.product_name) AS count
FROM 
  Ref_Payment_Methods pm
  JOIN Ref_Service_Types rt ON pm.payment_method_code = rt.service_type_code
  JOIN Products p ON rt.service_type_code = p.product_type_code
GROUP BY 
  pm.payment_method_code
ORDER BY 
  count DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

This query will return the payment method that is used the most often in all the invoices. The `COUNT(DISTINCT p.product_name)` counts the number of unique products for each payment method, and the `ORDER BY` clause sorts the results in descending order by the count. The `LIMIT 1` clause returns only the first row of the sorted result set.
