This query joins the required tables to get the first row of the result set, which corresponds to the party with the most popular form. The `COUNT(DISTINCT s.service_id)` counts the number of unique services for each party, and the `ORDER BY` clause sorts the result in descending order by the count. The `LIMIT 1` clause returns only the first row of the result set.
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT p1.email
FROM Parties p1
JOIN Party_Addresses pa ON p1.party_id = pa.party_id
JOIN Addresses a ON pa.address_id = a.address_id
JOIN Individuals i ON p1.individual_id = i.individual_id
JOIN Organizations o ON p1.organization_id = o.organization_id
JOIN Organizations_Contact_Individuals oc ON o.organization_id = oc.organization_id
JOIN Individuals_Contact_Individuals ic ON oc.individual_id = ic.individual_id
JOIN Services s ON p1.form_id = s.form_id
JOIN Party_Forms pf ON p1.party_id = pf.party_id
JOIN Party_Services ps ON pf.service_id = ps.service_id
WHERE pf.date_completed = (SELECT MAX(date_completed) FROM Party_Forms)
ORDER BY p1.email ASC;
```

This query joins the required tables to get the party emails associated with the party that is the most common. The `MAX(date_completed)` function is used to get the date of the most completed form for each party. The `DISTINCT` keyword is used to get only unique emails. The `ORDER BY` clause is used to sort the results in ascending order by email.
